  <!-- ========== 5. INSIDE IRAN (descriptive, ~200 words) ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="inside-iran">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Inside Iran
      <span class="meta">State-media decode · 200 words · verb-ban + forecast-cite rule applies</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="inside-iran">
      <div class="iran-media-badges">
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRNA</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRIB</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Press TV</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Fars</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Tasnim</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Kayhan</span>
      </div>
            <p>Iran's information environment since March 8 — when Mojtaba Khamenei was installed as Supreme Leader following Ali Khamenei's death in the February 28 US-Israeli strikes on Tehran — operates under a framework of managed legitimacy for an unproven leadership figure absent from public view. State media (IRNA, PressTV, Tasnim) frames the conflict as a defensive contest for sovereign rights: the Islamabad MOU's partial sanctions relief was presented domestically as validation of Iran's resistance posture rather than a concession; the MOU's July 7 collapse is attributed entirely to US violations of freedom-of-navigation commitments and failure to disburse the $300 billion reconstruction commitment before Iran's own obligations were due. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>
      <p>The IRGC's Persian Gulf Strait Authority toll operation receives no domestic framing as a revenue mechanism. IRNA presents it as legitimate sovereign prerogative: maritime security management and environmental monitoring services for which compensation is owed. Araghchi's August 2 characterization of Oman negotiations as in "final stages" (IRNA) is framed as Iran negotiating from strength, not from economic desperation under the Hormuz closure. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>
      <p>Mojtaba Khamenei's absence from public events since March 8 has generated no documented dissent in TIER1 sources. Iran's president's July 21 attribution to him of "greater access" in state strategy (Tasnim/Bloomberg) functions as a legitimizing narrative: decisions are sanctioned at the Supreme Leader level without requiring his physical appearance. State media avoids language framing the war as existential to the regime; infrastructure damage from US-Israeli strikes is framed as documented American aggression that obligates reconstruction compensation, not evidence of Iranian strategic failure. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>
      
    </div>
  </div>

